Chapter: WriteFileNoImpl

 write-file printed its content to the console and returned success. A program
 that saved a file was told it had saved a file, and nothing was written
 anywhere. Silent data loss, reported as success, in the one operation whose
 whole purpose is not to lose data.

 Then it refused honestly, with CDX2043, because Fat16 was read-only and there
 was nothing to point it at. The prose here said, in as many words: the day the
 write path exists, this test is the thing that has to change first.

 That day came. Fat16 writes now -- it allocates a cluster, chains it through
 every copy of the FAT, writes the bytes and commits a directory entry -- and
 the name write-file is gone from builtin-names and from the type environment
 entirely.

 What is left is the guarantee that outlives both the lie and the refusal: the
 compiler does not hand you a name it cannot keep. A chapter that wants to save
 a file cites Foreword chapter Fat16 and calls fat16-write-file, which works. A
 chapter that does not cite it -- like this one -- gets CDX3002 on the name, at
 the first opportunity, rather than an unresolved offset at the end of the
 build.

 An undefined name is a better outcome than a defined one that does nothing.

Section: Body
  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Write] Nothing = act
    write-file "/config/app.toml" "saved = true"
    print-line-uni "wrote it"
  end
